WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:02.102
- In the research for this book,

00:00:02.102 --> 00:00:06.773
I spoke to teenagers at
Newcomers High School

00:00:08.208 --> 00:00:10.010
in New York City, which is
a high school for immigrants

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:11.311
who have just arrived.

00:00:11.311 --> 00:00:13.046
And most of the kids I talked to

00:00:13.046 --> 00:00:16.750
were going to celebrate
their first Thanksgiving

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:18.551
in just a few weeks.

00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:23.690 align:start size:84% position:16%
And their enthusiasm
for the holiday

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:28.628
was very, it affected
me very profoundly.

00:00:28.628 --> 00:00:31.164 align:start size:81% position:19%
They identified
with the pilgrims.

00:00:32.332 --> 00:00:34.401
Now, let me give you
a couple of examples.

00:00:34.401 --> 00:00:38.805 align:start size:88% position:13%
One, a boy who
identified as a Tibetan,

00:00:38.805 --> 00:00:40.907 align:start size:84% position:16%
although that country
hasn't existed

00:00:40.907 --> 00:00:45.245
since China took
it over in 1950,

00:00:47.247 --> 00:00:48.715
he said he was a pilgrim.

00:00:48.715 --> 00:00:51.885 align:start size:88% position:13%
Why? Because his family,
like the pilgrims,

00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:55.422 align:start size:88% position:13%
came to America to
practice their religion.

00:00:55.422 --> 00:00:58.258
In China, he said,
he couldn't practice

00:00:58.258 --> 00:01:01.895
the Tibetan Buddhism
of the Dalai Lama.

00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:04.464 align:start size:88% position:13%
Then a little girl
spoke up, and she said,

00:01:04.464 --> 00:01:06.399
"Oh, that's like my family.

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:09.803
We came here from Egypt
because we're Copts,"

00:01:09.803 --> 00:01:14.140
and Copts are an early
version of Christianity,

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.276
an early sect of Christianity.

00:01:16.276 --> 00:01:19.779
So these kids really got it.

00:01:21.815 --> 00:01:25.952 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then a week or two
later, after Thanksgiving,

00:01:26.853 --> 00:01:28.922
I went to a Hanukkah party

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:32.725
of a group from my
husband's family.

00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:35.361
And I met a kid
who was attending

00:01:35.361 --> 00:01:39.265 align:start position:0%
one of the most prestigious
public high schools in America,

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:43.069
hearing that I wrote a book
on Thanksgiving, he started,

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:44.904
he said, "Oh, that's the holiday

00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:49.876 align:start size:97% position:3%
that we celebrate for
murdering all the Indians."

00:01:50.777 --> 00:01:52.045
And you know, I thought...

00:01:52.045 --> 00:01:54.914
- A prestigious high
school. Ugh. Okay.

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:56.549
- Yeah. So who got it right?

00:01:56.549 --> 00:01:59.786
(dramatic theme music)

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:07.327 line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin, and joining
me on this very special week

00:02:07.327 --> 00:02:09.462 line:0%
is a senior fellow at
the Hudson Institute,

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:12.699
author of "Thanksgiving:
The Holiday at the Heart

00:02:12.699 --> 00:02:14.567
of the American Experience,"

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:17.337
and the presenter in
the Prager U. video

00:02:17.337 --> 00:02:19.506
"Lincoln and Thanksgiving,"

00:02:19.506 --> 00:02:22.108 align:start size:97% position:3%
Melanie Kirkpatrick. Welcome
to "The Rubin Report."

00:02:22.108 --> 00:02:23.643
- It's wonderful to be with you,

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:25.979
especially on Thanksgiving week.

00:02:25.979 --> 00:02:27.914
- It seemed like
you were the person

00:02:27.914 --> 00:02:29.616
to talk to on Thanksgiving.

00:02:29.616 --> 00:02:31.484 align:start size:94% position:6%
I have a lot of questions
about Thanksgiving.

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:34.754 align:start size:97% position:3%
You're the, are you the
preeminent Thanksgiving expert

00:02:34.754 --> 00:02:36.222
in America?

00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:41.127 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, I don't want to sound
immodest, but perhaps so.

00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:42.495
- Pretty good. All right.

00:02:42.495 --> 00:02:44.230 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I have a lot of
questions about tryptophan,

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:46.032 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I think you
mostly want to focus

00:02:46.032 --> 00:02:49.002 align:start size:97% position:3%
on the history of
Thanksgiving. Is that correct?

00:02:49.002 --> 00:02:50.236
- Sounds like a good idea,

00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:53.473
though tryptophan has
come up, you know.

00:02:53.473 --> 00:02:56.109 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's one of the reasons
everybody loves Thanksgiving.

00:02:56.109 --> 00:02:57.677
It helps put you to sleep.

00:02:57.677 --> 00:02:59.712
- Puts you to sleep.
You unbuckle the pants.

00:02:59.712 --> 00:03:00.780
You're good to go. All right.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:02.315
So let's talk,

00:03:02.315 --> 00:03:04.217
because this actually is a
very special Thanksgiving,

00:03:04.217 --> 00:03:05.852
Thanksgiving 2021.

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:09.155 align:start size:97% position:3%
This is the 400th
anniversary of Thanksgiving.

00:03:09.155 --> 00:03:11.291
I feel like a lot of
people don't know that.

00:03:11.291 --> 00:03:12.926
So let's do a little 101.

00:03:12.926 --> 00:03:16.362 align:start size:88% position:13%
What happened 400
years ago back in 1621?

00:03:18.464 --> 00:03:19.832 line:0%
- The first thing I'll mention

00:03:19.832 --> 00:03:23.136 align:start position:0% line:0%
is that there are two
eyewitness accounts by pilgrims

00:03:23.136 --> 00:03:24.904 line:0%
of the first Thanksgiving,

00:03:26.306 --> 00:03:29.008 align:start size:97% position:3%
and neither one of them uses
the word "Thanksgiving,"

00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:30.476
which was kind of interesting.

00:03:30.476 --> 00:03:33.546
That's been imposed on
it over the centuries.

00:03:37.984 --> 00:03:39.819
But it dovetails with
the pilgrims' idea

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:41.187
of what a Thanksgiving is.

00:03:41.187 --> 00:03:44.924 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's a day for
expressing gratitude.

00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:47.160 align:start size:81% position:19%
It was always a
religious holiday,

00:03:48.561 --> 00:03:51.531
but the event of
1621 was probably

00:03:51.531 --> 00:03:53.933
more of a harvest festival.

00:03:53.933 --> 00:03:56.469 align:start size:97% position:3%
But that doesn't mean we
can't call it a Thanksgiving,

00:03:56.469 --> 00:04:00.673
because if you read those
two first-person accounts

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:04.577
by pilgrim Edward Winslow

00:04:04.577 --> 00:04:07.680 align:start size:97% position:3%
and William Bradford, the
governor of Plymouth Colony,

00:04:08.815 --> 00:04:12.852
they present an event
that was very much

00:04:12.852 --> 00:04:16.756
like the one we celebrate
today, at least in spirit.

00:04:16.756 --> 00:04:20.493
The spirit of gratitude
was at the forefront.

00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:25.465
It was the spirit of amity
with the Native Americans.

00:04:25.465 --> 00:04:28.268
It was a time of sharing.

00:04:28.268 --> 00:04:32.605
It was a time of fellowship,
and it was a time of peace.

00:04:32.605 --> 00:04:35.108 align:start size:81% position:19%
And one of the
things I object to

00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:41.381 align:start size:97% position:3%
is the modern-day, progressive
interpretation of that day

00:04:42.582 --> 00:04:46.653 line:0%
as the beginning of
a period, you know,

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:50.223 line:0%
the destruction of the
Native American peoples,

00:04:50.223 --> 00:04:51.958 line:0%
and that's completely wrong,

00:04:51.958 --> 00:04:55.862
because, if anything,
the first Thanksgiving

00:04:55.862 --> 00:05:00.300 align:start size:84% position:16%
points the way to
the multicultural
people we have become,

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:03.336 align:start size:94% position:6%
because the Native
Americans and the Indians

00:05:03.336 --> 00:05:07.473
were celebrating together,
peacefully and in harmony.

00:05:07.473 --> 00:05:10.310 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah. So it's hard for a
lot of people to believe that.

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:12.478
And I don't want to dwell on
it, because it's not true.

00:05:12.478 --> 00:05:13.880
You discuss it in the book.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:15.448 align:start size:84% position:16%
You discuss it in
the Prager U. Video.

00:05:15.448 --> 00:05:17.550 align:start size:94% position:6%
But can you just, just
for another minute or two,

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:19.185
just explore that a little bit,

00:05:19.185 --> 00:05:21.187
that, you know, people have
this idea that they were,

00:05:21.187 --> 00:05:23.623
they were just there to kill
each other or whatever it is,

00:05:23.623 --> 00:05:25.658 align:start size:88% position:13%
whatever the modern
version of all this is.

00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:27.794
But really, 400 years ago,

00:05:27.794 --> 00:05:31.230
the Native Americans and the
pilgrims were sitting down,

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:35.601
and they were saying, "Hey,
we can be thankful together

00:05:35.601 --> 00:05:37.770 align:start size:84% position:16%
on this land
peacefully together."

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.205
- Yes. Yes, they were.

00:05:39.205 --> 00:05:42.942
And the pilgrims had a
lot to be thankful for,

00:05:44.143 --> 00:05:46.579
for the assistance of
the Native Americans.

00:05:48.548 --> 00:05:51.050
They probably would
not have survived

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:54.320 align:start size:84% position:16%
had they not received
the instruction

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:57.490 align:start size:94% position:6%
from the Native Americans
on how to plant,

00:05:57.490 --> 00:06:01.761 align:start position:0%
where to find good hunting
grounds and good fishing waters.

00:06:01.761 --> 00:06:05.231
So, and, and they knew it.

00:06:06.666 --> 00:06:10.470 align:start size:97% position:3%
There were 52 pilgrims present
at that first Thanksgiving.

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:15.308 align:start size:97% position:3%
And most of them, only
about half of them were men.

00:06:15.308 --> 00:06:20.313 align:start size:88% position:13%
And yet there were 90
Indian warriors present.

00:06:22.148 --> 00:06:25.585 align:start size:97% position:3%
So you can see why they might
have been a little wary.

00:06:25.585 --> 00:06:28.755
And that was probably one
reason that they decided

00:06:29.655 --> 00:06:34.494
to have a shooting presentation.

00:06:34.494 --> 00:06:36.629
They showed the Native Americans

00:06:36.629 --> 00:06:39.499 align:start size:97% position:3%
how, you know, their guns
and how they could use them.

00:06:39.499 --> 00:06:42.268
And it might've been
in part a warning.

00:06:42.268 --> 00:06:45.138
But at the same time,

00:06:45.138 --> 00:06:49.609
they were very grateful for the
help of the Native Americans

00:06:49.609 --> 00:06:52.779
that made it possible
for them to survive.

00:06:52.779 --> 00:06:54.814 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So you tell the
story of Thanksgiving,

00:06:54.814 --> 00:06:56.983 align:start size:88% position:13%
because it didn't
exactly become a holiday

00:06:56.983 --> 00:06:58.518
right then and there,

00:06:58.518 --> 00:07:00.253
that there are two sort of
people that were integral

00:07:00.253 --> 00:07:01.387
to the story of Thanksgiving.

00:07:01.387 --> 00:07:03.156
One of the people we know well.

00:07:03.156 --> 00:07:05.591 align:start position:0%
One, we don't know as well,
although you just wrote a book,

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:07.226
I believe, about
the second person.

00:07:07.226 --> 00:07:08.928
So tell us about
those two people.

00:07:10.129 --> 00:07:11.764
- The first one is
President Lincoln,

00:07:12.932 --> 00:07:14.867
and the second is
Sarah Josepha Hale.

00:07:14.867 --> 00:07:19.205 align:start size:97% position:3%
And the link between the
two of them is Thanksgiving.

00:07:19.205 --> 00:07:24.010
Hale was the most influential
woman of the 19th Century.

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:29.015
She was editor of "Godey's
Lady's Book" for 50 years,

00:07:30.216 --> 00:07:33.119
and this was one of
the most widely read

00:07:33.119 --> 00:07:38.124 align:start size:97% position:3%
and widely circulated
magazines in the 19th Century.

00:07:39.459 --> 00:07:41.961 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it's kind of an amazing
story of how a widow

00:07:41.961 --> 00:07:46.966
with five small children rose
to such an exalted position,

00:07:47.567 --> 00:07:48.201
but she did.

00:07:49.902 --> 00:07:54.607 align:start size:97% position:3%
And she was very keen on
using, well, let me back up.

00:07:54.607 --> 00:07:57.310
She was born in 1788,

00:07:57.310 --> 00:08:00.680
and she believed
that while America

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:04.150 align:start size:97% position:3%
had been unified politically
through the revolution,

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:06.853
it was not unified culturally.

00:08:06.853 --> 00:08:11.357
And she set out to help
do that in her magazines.

00:08:11.357 --> 00:08:16.362
She published American authors
writing on American subjects,

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:19.632 align:start size:84% position:16%
which seems unusual
from the point of view

00:08:19.632 --> 00:08:21.567
of the 21st Century.

00:08:21.567 --> 00:08:25.972 align:start size:97% position:3%
But back then, it was
commonplace for publications

00:08:25.972 --> 00:08:30.309 align:start size:97% position:3%
to steal articles from
Britain or other publications

00:08:30.309 --> 00:08:34.146 align:start position:0%
and republish them without
credit in their own publication.

00:08:34.146 --> 00:08:36.482
But she had a different
vision for her magazine.

00:08:36.482 --> 00:08:39.952
She wanted to have
a focus on America

00:08:39.952 --> 00:08:42.588
and a focus on American authors.

00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:47.260 align:start size:94% position:6%
So she published such
authors as Edgar Allen Poe

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:49.529 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the young Harriet
Beecher Stowe.

00:08:49.529 --> 00:08:51.230
Many, many others.

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:56.235 align:start size:97% position:3%
But the idea of Thanksgiving
was a part of her vision

00:08:57.136 --> 00:09:00.873
of creating an American culture.

00:09:00.873 --> 00:09:03.809
Thanksgiving was very popular
in New England, of course,

00:09:03.809 --> 00:09:06.712
and she had grown up
loving the holiday.

00:09:06.712 --> 00:09:10.116 align:start size:97% position:3%
There were Thanksgivings
celebrated in the other states

00:09:10.116 --> 00:09:12.451
and territories as well,

00:09:13.653 --> 00:09:18.658
but the governors of the
states didn't coordinate

00:09:19.258 --> 00:09:20.026
on the holiday.

00:09:20.026 --> 00:09:21.494
Each was...

00:09:21.494 --> 00:09:24.897 align:start position:0%
And sometimes some states didn't
bother to celebrate at all.

00:09:24.897 --> 00:09:27.533 align:start size:88% position:13%
So you had a
situation, for example,

00:09:27.533 --> 00:09:31.437
in 1837, Hale was
living in Boston,

00:09:31.437 --> 00:09:33.973
which celebrated on one day.

00:09:33.973 --> 00:09:38.277 align:start position:0%
She was from New Hampshire,
which celebrated on another day.

00:09:38.277 --> 00:09:42.348 align:start size:97% position:3%
And her magazine was
headquartered in Philadelphia,

00:09:42.348 --> 00:09:45.718 align:start size:97% position:3%
and Pennsylvania wasn't
celebrating at all that year.

00:09:46.986 --> 00:09:50.790 align:start size:88% position:13%
So her vision was to
have Americans celebrate

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:53.893
on the same day every year.

00:09:53.893 --> 00:09:56.128
She thought that giving thanks,

00:09:56.128 --> 00:09:59.565 align:start size:88% position:13%
the idea of coming
together to give thanks

00:10:00.733 --> 00:10:05.037
as a nation would help.

00:10:06.205 --> 00:10:09.342 align:start size:88% position:13%
It had a higher moral
principle, she believed,

00:10:09.342 --> 00:10:11.677
and that it would bring
the country together

00:10:11.677 --> 00:10:13.179
in a positive way.

00:10:14.313 --> 00:10:17.116 align:start size:88% position:13%
And as the country moved
closer to civil war,

00:10:17.116 --> 00:10:21.220 align:start size:88% position:13%
she hoped that it
would prevent that war.

00:10:21.220 --> 00:10:23.356
Obviously, she didn't succeed,

00:10:23.356 --> 00:10:28.361
but in 1863, President
Lincoln took up her idea,

00:10:29.829 --> 00:10:32.331
which had been rejected by
four previous presidents,

00:10:35.001 --> 00:10:37.570
and decided to call a
national Thanksgiving.

00:10:37.570 --> 00:10:40.172
- So basically it
took over 200 years

00:10:40.172 --> 00:10:43.175
of sort of a mishmosh of,
you know, who's doing it,

00:10:43.175 --> 00:10:45.378
when are they doing it,
why are they doing it.

00:10:45.378 --> 00:10:48.347
And then finally Lincoln said,
okay, we're all gonna do it.

00:10:48.347 --> 00:10:49.782
And then did everyone
kind of fall in place

00:10:49.782 --> 00:10:52.251
and immediately
say, let's do it?

00:10:53.486 --> 00:10:55.354
- The South didn't want
to listen to Lincoln

00:10:55.354 --> 00:10:56.922
during the Civil War, of course,

00:10:56.922 --> 00:11:01.827 align:start size:94% position:6%
but the Union rallied very
positively to his call.

00:11:02.995 --> 00:11:07.233
And after the war, the
Southern states slowly,

00:11:08.434 --> 00:11:10.202
you know, one by one,
came together as well

00:11:10.202 --> 00:11:11.737
on Thanksgiving Day.

00:11:11.737 --> 00:11:12.938
- Yeah. One of the
things you mentioned

00:11:12.938 --> 00:11:15.508 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the Prager U. video
is that the people

00:11:15.508 --> 00:11:17.910
were actually eating the
same stuff, in many cases,

00:11:17.910 --> 00:11:18.844
that we eat now.

00:11:18.844 --> 00:11:21.213
It was turkey and pumpkin pie,

00:11:21.213 --> 00:11:22.314
which is sort of-

00:11:22.314 --> 00:11:23.749
- Yeah!

00:11:23.749 --> 00:11:24.717 align:start size:97% position:3%
- It's fun that we're
still eating the same stuff

00:11:24.717 --> 00:11:26.218
a couple hundred years after.

00:11:26.218 --> 00:11:30.656
- And, you know, that goes
back much farther than 1863.

00:11:30.656 --> 00:11:34.927 align:start size:84% position:16%
In the 1800s, I
found many examples

00:11:34.927 --> 00:11:39.932 align:start position:0%
of turkey and pumpkin pie
appearing on Thanksgiving menus.

00:11:40.700 --> 00:11:41.967
So, and those, you know,

00:11:41.967 --> 00:11:46.105 align:start size:81% position:19%
were native to the
country and so,

00:11:46.105 --> 00:11:49.775 align:start size:97% position:3%
to North America, at least,
so it's not surprising.

00:11:49.775 --> 00:11:53.779
There've been many changes in
the menu over the years too.

00:11:53.779 --> 00:11:57.483 align:start size:88% position:13%
For example, in New
England, chicken pot pie

00:11:57.483 --> 00:12:01.887
was a standard feature of
the Thanksgiving buffet.

00:12:01.887 --> 00:12:06.892
Venison also appeared on a
lot of Thanksgiving tables,

00:12:07.927 --> 00:12:10.963
and the tradition of pies was,

00:12:10.963 --> 00:12:12.498
you didn't have just one.

00:12:12.498 --> 00:12:17.503 align:start size:97% position:3%
You had an array, a big
selection of pies at the time.

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:20.840
So, but the, from the beginning,

00:12:20.840 --> 00:12:23.209 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think Thanksgiving
has been a feast.

00:12:24.343 --> 00:12:25.611 align:start size:81% position:19%
And that certainly
is true today.

00:12:25.611 --> 00:12:27.513
- Yeah. You know, I
don't want to focus

00:12:27.513 --> 00:12:29.782 align:start size:84% position:16%
on the cancel culture
aspect of this.

00:12:29.782 --> 00:12:31.250 align:start size:84% position:16%
We did a show on
Christopher Columbus

00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:32.918
a couple months ago,
and I try not to focus

00:12:32.918 --> 00:12:34.453
on all of that stuff.

00:12:34.453 --> 00:12:38.324
However, this is now a holiday
that is sort of under attack.

00:12:38.324 --> 00:12:41.127
It represents the
goodness of America

00:12:41.127 --> 00:12:43.062
and all the good things that
we should be thankful for.

00:12:43.062 --> 00:12:45.731
And there's an awful lot of
people that aren't thankful

00:12:45.731 --> 00:12:47.967 align:start size:81% position:19%
for these days,
or aren't thankful

00:12:47.967 --> 00:12:49.668
for everything we
have these days.

00:12:49.668 --> 00:12:53.172
What can we do to
push back on that?

00:12:53.172 --> 00:12:56.442
- Well, we can learn the
history of the holiday

00:12:57.343 --> 00:12:58.878
for one thing.

00:12:58.878 --> 00:13:03.783
The teaching of the holiday
has been poor over the years.

00:13:04.884 --> 00:13:07.686 align:start size:84% position:16%
Native Americans
understandably object

00:13:07.686 --> 00:13:11.490 align:start size:84% position:16%
to what, at least
when I was growing up,

00:13:11.490 --> 00:13:15.194 align:start size:97% position:3%
the focus on the
generosity of the pilgrims,

00:13:15.194 --> 00:13:18.531 align:start size:94% position:6%
rather than on the
generosity and assistance

00:13:18.531 --> 00:13:19.732
of the Native Americans.

00:13:19.732 --> 00:13:22.701 align:start size:88% position:13%
So we can get that right
in teaching children

00:13:22.701 --> 00:13:23.869
about the holiday.

00:13:24.904 --> 00:13:26.272
I also think we can

00:13:29.408 --> 00:13:33.846
listen to immigrants when it
comes to their understanding

00:13:33.846 --> 00:13:35.681
of the holiday.

00:13:35.681 --> 00:13:37.550
Thanksgiving is
a rite of passage

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:39.418
for most immigrants to America.

00:13:39.418 --> 00:13:43.088
It's part of joining
the American family.

00:13:43.088 --> 00:13:46.358
This is a very positive thing.

00:13:46.358 --> 00:13:47.960 align:start size:84% position:16%
And in the research
for this book,

00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:52.965
I spoke to teenagers at
Newcomers High School

00:13:53.732 --> 00:13:55.367
in New York City,

00:13:55.367 --> 00:13:57.536 align:start size:84% position:16%
which is a high school
for immigrants who
have just arrived.

00:13:57.536 --> 00:13:59.271
And most of the kids I talked to

00:13:59.271 --> 00:14:02.875
were going to celebrate
their first Thanksgiving

00:14:02.875 --> 00:14:04.743
in just a few weeks.

00:14:04.743 --> 00:14:09.748
And their enthusiasm
for the holiday was,

00:14:12.585 --> 00:14:14.787
it affected me very profoundly.

00:14:14.787 --> 00:14:17.289 align:start size:81% position:19%
They identified
with the pilgrims.

00:14:18.457 --> 00:14:20.492 line:0%
Now, let me give you
a couple of examples.

00:14:20.492 --> 00:14:24.897 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
One, a boy who
identified as a Tibetan,

00:14:24.897 --> 00:14:26.999 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
although that country
hasn't existed

00:14:26.999 --> 00:14:31.337
since China took
it over in 1950,

00:14:32.238 --> 00:14:34.540
he said he was a pilgrim.

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:37.943 align:start size:88% position:13%
Why? Because his family,
like the pilgrims,

00:14:37.943 --> 00:14:42.147
came to America to practice
their religion in China.

00:14:42.147 --> 00:14:46.185 align:start size:97% position:3%
He said he couldn't practice
the Tibetan Buddhism

00:14:46.185 --> 00:14:48.087
of the Dalai Lama.

00:14:48.087 --> 00:14:49.989
Then a little girl spoke up,

00:14:49.989 --> 00:14:52.558 align:start size:84% position:16%
and she said, "Oh,
that's like my family.

00:14:52.558 --> 00:14:55.995 align:start size:88% position:13%
We came here from Egypt,
because we're Copts,"

00:14:55.995 --> 00:15:00.299
and Copts are an early
version of Christianity,

00:15:00.299 --> 00:15:02.401
an early sect of Christianity.

00:15:02.401 --> 00:15:05.905
So these kids really got it.

00:15:07.473 --> 00:15:11.977
And then a week or two
later after Thanksgiving,

00:15:12.978 --> 00:15:15.014
I went to a Hanukkah party

00:15:15.014 --> 00:15:19.118
of a group from my
husband's family.

00:15:19.118 --> 00:15:21.287
And I met a kid
who was attending

00:15:21.287 --> 00:15:25.391 align:start position:0%
one of the most prestigious
public high schools in America,

00:15:25.391 --> 00:15:29.161
hearing that I wrote a book
on Thanksgiving, he started,

00:15:29.161 --> 00:15:32.197
he said, "Oh, that's the
holiday that we celebrate

00:15:33.532 --> 00:15:36.035
for murdering all the Indians."

00:15:36.035 --> 00:15:37.269
And you know, I thought-

00:15:37.269 --> 00:15:41.006
- A prestigious high
school. Ugh. Okay.

00:15:41.006 --> 00:15:42.808
- Yeah. So who got it right?

00:15:42.808 --> 00:15:45.544
So education is, is one thing.

00:15:45.544 --> 00:15:48.514 align:start size:97% position:3%
And let me end on a
positive note here, though.

00:15:48.514 --> 00:15:53.018 align:start position:0%
You know, yes, progressives
are out to cancel Thanksgiving,

00:15:54.186 --> 00:15:57.423
but I don't think they
have a hope of success,

00:15:58.624 --> 00:16:01.527
because Americans like
this holidays so much.

00:16:01.527 --> 00:16:05.798 align:start size:97% position:3%
And even if they don't fully
grasp the history of it,

00:16:05.798 --> 00:16:10.803 align:start size:97% position:3%
or even if they pay lip
service to the cancel culture,

00:16:12.271 --> 00:16:14.073
they're not going to stop
celebrating Thanksgiving.

00:16:14.073 --> 00:16:19.078
And also, I think a lot
of very positive aspects

00:16:20.245 --> 00:16:21.513
of Thanksgiving are
going to continue.

00:16:21.513 --> 00:16:23.649
Families are going
to come together.

00:16:23.649 --> 00:16:27.219 align:start size:94% position:6%
Americans have a tradition
of inviting people

00:16:27.219 --> 00:16:29.555
who have no place to
go for Thanksgiving.

00:16:29.555 --> 00:16:34.560 align:start position:0%
And most of all, I love the
idea of Thanksgiving generosity,

00:16:35.928 --> 00:16:39.832 align:start size:94% position:6%
which, the idea that
Thanksgiving is a holiday

00:16:41.133 --> 00:16:42.434
when we think carefully

00:16:42.434 --> 00:16:46.038 align:start size:88% position:13%
about those who are
less fortunate among us.

00:16:46.038 --> 00:16:48.507 align:start size:84% position:16%
And you think of
all the non-profits,

00:16:49.942 --> 00:16:52.044
all the non-religious, all
the religious organizations,

00:16:52.044 --> 00:16:54.213
and also all the individuals

00:16:54.213 --> 00:16:57.216 align:start size:88% position:13%
who make sure that
people who are in jail,

00:16:57.216 --> 00:16:59.084
people who are in hospital,

00:16:59.084 --> 00:17:02.921
and people who have
no family at all

00:17:02.921 --> 00:17:07.026
have an opportunity to have
a good Thanksgiving dinner.

00:17:07.026 --> 00:17:11.663 align:start size:88% position:13%
And this is a real
hallmark of the holiday

00:17:11.663 --> 00:17:14.299
and of American generosity.

00:17:14.299 --> 00:17:15.401
- Yeah, and I have to
say that, you know,

00:17:15.401 --> 00:17:16.869
over the last couple of years,

00:17:16.869 --> 00:17:20.472
as some of our freedoms have
felt like they're disappearing,

00:17:20.472 --> 00:17:23.242 align:start size:97% position:3%
or at least being challenged
in very bizarre ways,

00:17:23.242 --> 00:17:24.910 align:start size:84% position:16%
I've definitely become
more appreciative.

00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:26.078
I've always loved Thanksgiving,

00:17:26.078 --> 00:17:28.047 align:start size:84% position:16%
because of the
food and the smells

00:17:28.047 --> 00:17:30.282
and the people and the
family and all that.

00:17:30.282 --> 00:17:31.784
You mentioned at the beginning

00:17:31.784 --> 00:17:33.285 align:start size:97% position:3%
that this was really thought
of as a religious holiday

00:17:33.285 --> 00:17:35.821
at first, which is
sort of not the way

00:17:35.821 --> 00:17:37.589
I think most people think of it,

00:17:37.589 --> 00:17:39.691
because they think of it sort
of as our main secular holiday

00:17:39.691 --> 00:17:40.959
in America.

00:17:40.959 --> 00:17:42.461
Can you talk about
that a little bit?

00:17:42.461 --> 00:17:44.063
- Yeah. I think a better
way to think of it, Dave,

00:17:44.063 --> 00:17:49.068
is that it is the only holiday

00:17:50.335 --> 00:17:52.905
probably anywhere in the world

00:17:52.905 --> 00:17:57.242 align:start size:84% position:16%
that is open to all
religions and to none.

00:17:59.278 --> 00:18:03.215
So it's, you know, doesn't
matter what your religion is.

00:18:03.215 --> 00:18:06.051 align:start size:84% position:16%
Doesn't matter
where you came from.

00:18:08.253 --> 00:18:10.456
It is every American's holiday.

00:18:10.456 --> 00:18:13.959
So, yeah, it used to be,

00:18:13.959 --> 00:18:16.161
even when I was growing up,

00:18:16.161 --> 00:18:18.430
that people went to
church, you know,

00:18:18.430 --> 00:18:20.799 align:start size:97% position:3%
the night before Thanksgiving
or Thanksgiving morning.

00:18:20.799 --> 00:18:23.502
That doesn't happen
very much anymore.

00:18:23.502 --> 00:18:27.473 align:start size:88% position:13%
But if there is a day
that a family says grace

00:18:27.473 --> 00:18:31.243 align:start size:84% position:16%
before a meal, it
is Thanksgiving Day.

00:18:31.243 --> 00:18:34.246 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah. So you mentioned
before that Abraham Lincoln,

00:18:34.246 --> 00:18:35.948
obviously first, we
have the Civil War,

00:18:35.948 --> 00:18:39.651 align:start position:0%
so now he's got to get the
South to kind of come on board,

00:18:39.651 --> 00:18:41.086
all of this.

00:18:41.086 --> 00:18:43.422 align:start position:0%
Was everybody immediately
celebrating Thanksgiving after,

00:18:43.422 --> 00:18:45.390
or did it really take some time?

00:18:45.390 --> 00:18:49.027
- It took a while. I don't
recall off the top of my head

00:18:49.027 --> 00:18:52.064 align:start size:81% position:19%
the dates when the
Southern states

00:18:52.064 --> 00:18:54.733
individually began to celebrate,

00:18:54.733 --> 00:18:58.504 align:start size:84% position:16%
but certainly by
the end of the 19th,

00:18:58.504 --> 00:19:00.172
beginning of the 20th Century,

00:19:00.172 --> 00:19:03.108
there was unity in the states.

00:19:03.108 --> 00:19:04.776
And let me tell
you a funny story

00:19:04.776 --> 00:19:09.648 align:start size:84% position:16%
about what happened
in the 1930s.

00:19:09.648 --> 00:19:13.886
During the Depression,
FDR had the dumb idea

00:19:13.886 --> 00:19:16.655
that if he moved the
date of Thanksgiving

00:19:16.655 --> 00:19:18.157
to allow more time

00:19:18.157 --> 00:19:21.627
between Thanksgiving
Day and Christmas,

00:19:21.627 --> 00:19:23.529
it would help the economy.

00:19:23.529 --> 00:19:27.666 align:start size:97% position:3%
His theory was that Americans
would have more days

00:19:27.666 --> 00:19:29.067
to go Christmas shopping,

00:19:29.067 --> 00:19:31.503
and that would be a
boost to the holiday.

00:19:31.503 --> 00:19:34.773
Well, I think Americans would
have liked to spend more money

00:19:34.773 --> 00:19:37.643
on Christmas presents, but
they didn't have the money.

00:19:37.643 --> 00:19:38.577
- [Dave] Right.

00:19:38.577 --> 00:19:41.480
- So the idea flopped, but,

00:19:41.480 --> 00:19:43.115
and for three days though,

00:19:43.115 --> 00:19:48.120
Americans had to either
celebrate on the new day,

00:19:49.555 --> 00:19:51.823
which was a week earlier
than the traditional date,

00:19:51.823 --> 00:19:55.027
or celebrate on the
traditional date.

00:19:55.027 --> 00:19:58.297
And the country was
split about 50-50.

00:20:00.299 --> 00:20:01.867
And the holiday began known as,

00:20:01.867 --> 00:20:04.937 align:start size:88% position:13%
became known as the
Democratic Thanksgiving

00:20:04.937 --> 00:20:07.206
and the Republican Thanksgiving,

00:20:07.206 --> 00:20:08.707
which was very funny,

00:20:08.707 --> 00:20:11.577 align:start size:97% position:3%
but there were a few states
that had the best idea of all.

00:20:11.577 --> 00:20:13.812
They celebrated on both days.

00:20:13.812 --> 00:20:16.648 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I know Texas and
Colorado were among them.

00:20:16.648 --> 00:20:18.684 align:start size:81% position:19%
I've forgotten
what the third is.

00:20:20.085 --> 00:20:25.023
But then in 1941, it was
clear that Roosevelt's idea

00:20:27.125 --> 00:20:29.595 align:start size:88% position:13%
for a new date for
Thanksgiving was a flop,

00:20:29.595 --> 00:20:34.533 align:start size:94% position:6%
and Congress finally acted
and passed a resolution

00:20:34.533 --> 00:20:38.036 align:start size:84% position:16%
naming the fourth
Thursday of November

00:20:39.238 --> 00:20:41.873 align:start size:81% position:19%
as the official
Thanksgiving date,

00:20:41.873 --> 00:20:46.612 align:start size:88% position:13%
and finally Thanksgiving
was written into law,

00:20:47.813 --> 00:20:50.182
and we've celebrated on
that date ever since.

00:20:50.182 --> 00:20:51.717
- As a Thanksgiving historian,

00:20:51.717 --> 00:20:55.687
does it madden you that
Friday after Thanksgiving

00:20:55.687 --> 00:20:57.589
has now become Black Friday,

00:20:57.589 --> 00:20:59.691
which is now leaking
into Thanksgiving?

00:20:59.691 --> 00:21:02.361
I mean, the new thing is now
that the stores open actually

00:21:02.361 --> 00:21:04.896
on Thanksgiving Day, the
one day we're supposed

00:21:04.896 --> 00:21:07.666 align:start size:97% position:3%
to set aside to be thankful,
and we've allowed it

00:21:07.666 --> 00:21:10.435 align:start size:84% position:16%
to become this sort
of commercial madness.

00:21:10.435 --> 00:21:14.539 align:start position:0%
- Yeah, I don't like the leakage
into Thanksgiving Thursday,

00:21:15.874 --> 00:21:16.808
I say.

00:21:16.808 --> 00:21:19.578
But I don't mind Black Friday.

00:21:19.578 --> 00:21:23.615
I think it's actually a sign
of the health of our economy,

00:21:23.615 --> 00:21:25.851 align:start size:84% position:16%
the prosperity of
the American people.

00:21:25.851 --> 00:21:28.620 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we should give thanks.
We should pay thanks.

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:30.289
Give thanks for that.

00:21:30.289 --> 00:21:31.690 align:start size:84% position:16%
- How did football-?
- I also-

00:21:31.690 --> 00:21:32.524
- Oh, go ahead.

00:21:32.524 --> 00:21:33.358
- Well, football.

00:21:33.358 --> 00:21:34.693
(she chuckles)

00:21:34.693 --> 00:21:36.161
What about football?

00:21:36.161 --> 00:21:37.829 align:start position:0%
- Yeah. How did football become
a part of this whole thing?

00:21:37.829 --> 00:21:39.998 align:start size:94% position:6%
I thought football was
supposed to be on Sundays.

00:21:43.135 --> 00:21:45.003
- Well, I am not
a fan of football,

00:21:45.003 --> 00:21:48.907
and this was the hardest
chapter for me to write.

00:21:48.907 --> 00:21:51.410
But I had a lot, you know,
I did a lot of research,

00:21:51.410 --> 00:21:52.444
and I got it.

00:21:53.879 --> 00:21:58.784 align:start size:97% position:3%
As you may recall, the
first American football game

00:21:58.784 --> 00:22:03.388
was played by Princeton
and Rutgers in 1869.

00:22:04.723 --> 00:22:08.427 align:start position:0%
And then in the 70s, Princeton,
which is my alma mater,

00:22:08.427 --> 00:22:09.928
I will say,

00:22:11.830 --> 00:22:16.835 align:start size:97% position:3%
and Yale played a Thanksgiving
Day football game.

00:22:17.703 --> 00:22:19.805
It was their championship game.

00:22:19.805 --> 00:22:23.542
That moved to New York
City in the late 1870s

00:22:23.542 --> 00:22:27.312 align:start size:88% position:13%
or around 1880, and
New Yorkers took it up.

00:22:27.312 --> 00:22:29.981 align:start size:84% position:16%
Even if they couldn't
go to the game,

00:22:29.981 --> 00:22:32.517
they came out in force,
walking the streets.

00:22:32.517 --> 00:22:34.786 align:start size:84% position:16%
They would go up and
down Fifth Avenue

00:22:34.786 --> 00:22:37.522
wearing orange and
black for Princeton

00:22:37.522 --> 00:22:38.990
or blue for Yale.

00:22:40.425 --> 00:22:43.962
And they really adopted this
football championship game

00:22:43.962 --> 00:22:45.597
in a big way.

00:22:45.597 --> 00:22:49.167 align:start size:97% position:3%
And of course, New Yorkers
have always been trendsetters,

00:22:49.167 --> 00:22:52.537
and the rest of the country
began to take up the idea

00:22:52.537 --> 00:22:55.040
of Thanksgiving Day football.

00:22:55.040 --> 00:22:57.876
A lot of towns would have,

00:22:57.876 --> 00:23:00.746 align:start size:97% position:3%
town high schools would
have their championship game

00:23:00.746 --> 00:23:04.483
on Thanksgiving morning and
then go home for dinner.

00:23:05.650 --> 00:23:10.455
And then I guess some
people had the idea

00:23:10.455 --> 00:23:13.959
in the early 20th Century
that this would be good

00:23:13.959 --> 00:23:15.927
for pro football too.

00:23:15.927 --> 00:23:19.097 align:start size:84% position:16%
And the Detroit Tigers
were the first team

00:23:19.097 --> 00:23:22.801
to play a Thanksgiving Day game,

00:23:22.801 --> 00:23:24.803 align:start size:84% position:16%
which they, of
course, still play.

00:23:26.238 --> 00:23:30.642
And then the Dallas Cowboys
took it up at some point.

00:23:32.778 --> 00:23:34.045
And now there's a third team,

00:23:34.045 --> 00:23:37.649
which I think changes
every year that plays.

00:23:39.251 --> 00:23:41.720 align:start size:94% position:6%
But Thanksgiving football
was controversial

00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:43.989
in the 19th Century.

00:23:43.989 --> 00:23:47.325
Ministers and rabbis
and others complained

00:23:47.325 --> 00:23:50.262
that it took, it
diverted attention

00:23:50.262 --> 00:23:52.931 align:start size:84% position:16%
from the true
meaning of the day.

00:23:52.931 --> 00:23:57.869 align:start size:88% position:13%
But, you know, I, I
think we can have turkey

00:23:57.869 --> 00:24:02.073 align:start size:94% position:6%
and football and gratitude
all in the same day.

00:24:02.073 --> 00:24:03.442
We can multitask.

00:24:03.442 --> 00:24:04.609
- Right, right.

00:24:04.609 --> 00:24:05.911 align:start size:88% position:13%
Although I'm sure
there were some people,

00:24:05.911 --> 00:24:07.446
of course, that probably felt

00:24:07.446 --> 00:24:10.148
that people shouldn't be, you
know, forced to work that day

00:24:10.148 --> 00:24:11.750
or that sort of thing too,

00:24:11.750 --> 00:24:13.218
but you think we can
fit all the things.

00:24:13.218 --> 00:24:14.986 align:start size:97% position:3%
What are some of the crazier
traditions you've heard?

00:24:14.986 --> 00:24:16.421
I assume that there's all sorts

00:24:16.421 --> 00:24:20.592 align:start position:0%
of different Thanksgiving
traditions all over the country.

00:24:20.592 --> 00:24:23.462
- Gosh. Yeah, there
are, but you know,

00:24:23.462 --> 00:24:25.964
the meal is the number one,

00:24:27.165 --> 00:24:28.733
is the number one tradition,

00:24:28.733 --> 00:24:32.137
and Sarah Josepha Hale,

00:24:32.137 --> 00:24:34.206
who I mentioned is the godmother

00:24:34.206 --> 00:24:36.107
of the modern-day Thanksgiving,

00:24:38.944 --> 00:24:41.279 align:start size:84% position:16%
she was the first
one to bring recipes,

00:24:41.279 --> 00:24:45.317 align:start size:84% position:16%
to put recipes in a
magazine or newspaper,

00:24:45.317 --> 00:24:47.118
to create a recipe section.

00:24:47.118 --> 00:24:50.622
And she published
lots of recipes

00:24:50.622 --> 00:24:53.158
culled from around the country

00:24:53.158 --> 00:24:55.293
for Thanksgiving Day feasts.

00:24:57.729 --> 00:25:01.166 line:0%
And you know, you hear, I
heard of some crazy pies

00:25:01.166 --> 00:25:02.634 line:0%
like sweet potato pie.

00:25:02.634 --> 00:25:04.035 line:0%
I had never heard of that,

00:25:04.035 --> 00:25:06.438 line:0%
but apparently it's
popular in the South.

00:25:08.106 --> 00:25:10.675 align:start size:88% position:13%
And there's a pie
called Marlborough pie,

00:25:10.675 --> 00:25:14.379
which is a kind of
apple lemon pie,

00:25:14.379 --> 00:25:17.516
which was very popular
in the 19th Century.

00:25:18.617 --> 00:25:20.385
But crazy customs.

00:25:20.385 --> 00:25:23.121
A lot of people go,
this isn't crazy,

00:25:23.121 --> 00:25:25.323
but a lot of people
go around the table

00:25:25.323 --> 00:25:28.860
and ask every individual

00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:31.897
to say something that
he is grateful for.

00:25:31.897 --> 00:25:34.332
- [Dave] Yep, we do
that. We do that.

00:25:34.332 --> 00:25:36.167
- Other families read,

00:25:37.335 --> 00:25:39.804 align:start size:84% position:16%
find something to read
about Thanksgiving

00:25:39.804 --> 00:25:42.908
for the family table.

00:25:44.309 --> 00:25:46.278
And I actually, at the back
of my Thanksgiving book,

00:25:46.278 --> 00:25:48.480 align:start size:84% position:16%
include readings for
Thanksgiving Day.

00:25:48.480 --> 00:25:51.416
And they're short,
very short, readings

00:25:51.416 --> 00:25:55.654 align:start position:0%
from over 400 years of American
history about Thanksgiving.

00:25:57.322 --> 00:26:00.692
And then there's a custom
that my family has adopted.

00:26:00.692 --> 00:26:04.062
I won't call it a crazy custom,
but it's a different custom.

00:26:04.062 --> 00:26:06.865
It's called "Five
Kernels of Corn."

00:26:08.133 --> 00:26:10.669
And it dates back to
the late 19th Century.

00:26:12.137 --> 00:26:16.041
People would put five kernels
of dried corn on their table.

00:26:16.041 --> 00:26:21.046
And the corn represented the
generosity of the native people

00:26:22.414 --> 00:26:26.551 align:start size:88% position:13%
who taught the pilgrims
how to grow corn.

00:26:26.551 --> 00:26:30.956 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it also represented the
starving time of the Plymouth,

00:26:30.956 --> 00:26:34.993 align:start size:97% position:3%
of the pilgrims in
Plymouth Colony, when they,

00:26:36.728 --> 00:26:40.031
legend says, were given
only five kernels of corn

00:26:40.031 --> 00:26:42.100
to eat every day.

00:26:42.100 --> 00:26:44.069
So, I like that.

00:26:44.069 --> 00:26:47.005 align:start size:84% position:16%
I like that idea.
Five kernels of corn.

00:26:47.005 --> 00:26:50.342 align:start size:97% position:3%
- How does our Thanksgiving,
some of the traditions,

00:26:50.342 --> 00:26:51.810
the foods, all that,
compare, you know,

00:26:51.810 --> 00:26:54.479 align:start size:84% position:16%
like we just had
Canadian Thanksgiving

00:26:54.479 --> 00:26:55.647
was a couple of weeks ago.

00:26:55.647 --> 00:26:57.983 align:start size:88% position:13%
I assume other countries
have some version,

00:26:57.983 --> 00:27:00.285 align:start size:97% position:3%
maybe not called
Thanksgiving, but you know,

00:27:00.285 --> 00:27:03.855
sort of a more secular day
of thanks type of thing.

00:27:03.855 --> 00:27:07.993 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, most of the
Thanksgivings around the world

00:27:07.993 --> 00:27:12.097
are religious in nature, or
at least originated as such.

00:27:12.097 --> 00:27:14.532
China has a kind
of Thanksgiving.

00:27:14.532 --> 00:27:16.768
It's more of a harvest festival.

00:27:18.169 --> 00:27:21.406
So do the two Koreas.

00:27:21.406 --> 00:27:24.075
Brazil has a Thanksgiving

00:27:24.075 --> 00:27:26.945
that is modeled on the
American Thanksgiving.

00:27:26.945 --> 00:27:31.950
The ambassador from Brazil
to America in the late 1940s

00:27:32.684 --> 00:27:34.185
loved Thanksgiving.

00:27:34.185 --> 00:27:37.656 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so he went home and
took it with him to Brazil,

00:27:37.656 --> 00:27:39.724
and they now have
such a holiday.

00:27:39.724 --> 00:27:42.727 align:start size:84% position:16%
And Canada's holiday
is interesting.

00:27:43.795 --> 00:27:46.331 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's on our Columbus
Day weekend.

00:27:50.869 --> 00:27:53.338 align:start size:88% position:13%
The idea of celebrating
Thanksgiving

00:27:53.338 --> 00:27:58.343
was carried to Canada by
British loyalists, who,

00:27:59.344 --> 00:28:01.212
American loyalists, excuse me,

00:28:01.212 --> 00:28:05.850 align:start size:97% position:3%
who fled during the revolution
to usually Nova Scotia.

00:28:07.652 --> 00:28:11.556
And because there was a close
tie between Boston and Halifax

00:28:11.556 --> 00:28:12.824
at the time,

00:28:12.824 --> 00:28:15.160
and they took the
holiday with them,

00:28:15.160 --> 00:28:18.930
and it spread around
the country of Canada.

00:28:18.930 --> 00:28:22.367 align:start size:97% position:3%
It doesn't have the same
meaning for Canadians, though,

00:28:22.367 --> 00:28:25.804 align:start size:94% position:6%
that it has for Americans,
because it's not,

00:28:25.804 --> 00:28:28.473 align:start size:94% position:6%
it doesn't have an
association with anything

00:28:28.473 --> 00:28:30.241
in the Canadian history.

00:28:31.443 --> 00:28:35.747
So while Canadians enjoy
the day and have a meal,

00:28:35.747 --> 00:28:38.383 align:start size:84% position:16%
a harvest meal that's
similar to ours,

00:28:39.684 --> 00:28:43.521 align:start size:97% position:3%
they don't feel the
connection to it that we feel.

00:28:43.521 --> 00:28:44.723
- Interesting.

00:28:44.723 --> 00:28:46.958 align:start size:88% position:13%
So it really was an
export from us to them.

00:28:46.958 --> 00:28:47.792
- Yeah.

00:28:47.792 --> 00:28:48.626
- In that sense.

00:28:48.626 --> 00:28:49.861
- Yeah. Yep.

00:28:49.861 --> 00:28:52.363
- Interesting. What else
do people need to know

00:28:52.363 --> 00:28:54.499
on this Thanksgiving
before I let them dive

00:28:54.499 --> 00:28:56.301
into their turkey and stuffing

00:28:56.301 --> 00:28:59.037
and all that good stuff,
their sweet potato pie?

00:29:00.238 --> 00:29:03.074
- Well, I, in response
